The COVID-19 pandemic has exacerbated existing disparities in higher education, disproportionately affecting students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds. This study examines the impact of pandemic-related school closures on the academic performance of U.S. college students during remote learning. Utilizing American College Testing (ACT) scores from 2019 and 2020, we analyze changes in student performance across various demographic groups. We find that students with the most limited prior access to higher education experienced the steepest declines in ACT scores due to school closures. Specifically, Black and Hispanic students, as well as those from lower-income families, demonstrated greater academic deterioration than their White and higher-income peers. These results indicate that pandemic disruptions have intensified pre-existing inequalities in U.S. higher education. \textit{JEL Codes: I21, I12, J15} \textit{Keywords: Pandemic, School Closures, Inequality, ACT Scores}

This paper investigates the relationship between the digital divide and intergenerational mobility in Spain. We define the digital divide as the disparity in access to and usage of Information and Communication Technologies (ICT) between generations. By linking the 2001 Spanish Household Survey with the Spanish Census of Population and Housing, we measure this divide using the proportion of households with a computer and internet connection. We categorize the divide into three types: access, usage, and the digital opportunity gap. While access and usage divides are negatively associated with mobility, the digital opportunity gap is positively associated with it.

